IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pild-dates`

-- Personal Injuries (Liabilities and Damages) Act 2003 (Northern Territory) -- the Territory's civil liability Act for
-- personal injury: which claims it reaches (ss 4, 5), who is not liable (volunteers, food donors, good Samaritans,
-- occupiers against intruders, injured offenders: ss 7-10), apologies (ss 11-13), contributory negligence and
-- intoxication (ss 14-17), institutional liability for child abuse (Part 3A), the limits on damages (Part 4: earnings cap,
-- future loss, gratuitous care, non-pecuniary loss by impairment, interest, structured settlements), and damages awarded
-- to offenders in custody and what their victims can reach (Part 4A).
--
-- SEAT: the injured person who brings the claim (the claimant); for Part 3A the person abused as a child; for Part 4A
-- both the person in custody who sues for assault, battery or false imprisonment, and the victim who sues that offender.
-- "I was hurt. Does this Act cover my claim? Can the person who hurt me escape liability? How much can I get for pain and
-- suffering? Will my damages be cut because I had been drinking?" Every satisfaction, finding or discretion of the court
-- is asked as an input, never decided.
--
-- Edition: reprint REPP078, as in force at 25 March 2024, from legislation.nt.gov.au (retrieved 6 November 2026), as
-- deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/Personal-Injuries-Liabilities-and-Damages-Act-2003-NT-current-reprint-REPP078.txt.
-- No regulations under the Act are deposited: the s 22 discount rate (5%) and the s 30 interest rate default are the
-- Act's own; amounts are in monetary units (their dollar value is fixed outside this Act, under the Revenue Units Act
-- 2009, not deposited).
--
-- NOT ENCODED: ss 1, 2, 6 (machinery); s 11 (purpose); s 17A management member and proper respondent (used in
-- words only); s 17D (the duty, stated in the answers of ss 17E, 17F); s 17E(3), 17F(3), 17G(2) (matters the court may or
-- must weigh: folded into the court's finding, an input); s 17E(4), (5), 17F(4), (7), 17G(4) (stated in the answers);
-- ss 17M(1), (2), (4), 17N (trustees' powers; s 17M(3) is stated); s 17Q(2)-(4), 17S (the court's procedural powers and
-- rules); s 24 (purpose); s 26 (evidence of impairment: the degree is an input); s 31 (definition, stated); s 32J(1)-(4),
-- (6), (7) (the public entity's notice: stated in the victim's answer); s 32K(3)-(6) (stated in the answer); s 32L;
-- s 32N; Parts 5, 6, 7 (regulations and transitional), save s 36 (applied to s 7A) and s 35(3) (noted).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the claimant's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Personal Injuries (Liabilities and Damages) Act 2003 -- the claimant`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with an `Answer`: either `answered` with the value,
-- or `not covered` with the reason.
DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    `"volunteer" includes a person paid no more than an amount prescribed by the Regulations (s 7(7)(b)); the Regulations are not deposited`
    `"community work" includes work for a purpose prescribed by regulation (s 7(7)(a)(viii)); the Regulations are not deposited`
    `"grocery product" includes a product prescribed by regulation (s 7A(4)(d)); the Regulations are not deposited`
    `"individual associated with an institution" is an "includes" list, and further individuals may be prescribed (s 17C(1)(e)); the Regulations are not deposited and the Act does not say whether others count`
    `the Table in section 27(3)(c) lists whole percentages from 10% to 14%, and section 27(3)(b) starts at 15%; a degree between two of them is not provided for`
    `section 27(3)(b) ends at 84% and section 27(3)(a) starts at 85%; a degree between them is not provided for`
    `only a victim notified in writing under section 32J(1)(a) is given the right to ask for details (s 32J(4)); the Act does not speak to others`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

-- Dates. The endnotes (List of legislation): the Act commenced on 1 May 2003; the Amendment Act 2010 (which inserted
-- s 7A) on 15 December 2010. The Amendment Act 2022 (No. 13 of 2022) commenced in three steps: "pt 2: 3 June 2022; pt 3:
-- 30 September 2022; rem: 1 January 2023". The endnotes do not say which Part of that Act inserted Part 4A Divisions 1-3
-- (its s 6); the numbering of its sections (ss 4-9 amending ss 3, 4, Part 4A Divisions 1-3, the long title, s 27, s 28;
-- ss 10-12 amending s 3, inserting Part 3A, and the long title again) points to Part 2, so section 32B is taken to have
-- commenced on 3 June 2022. Inferred, not read: see NOTES.md.
`the commencement of this Act` MEANS `the day` 1 5 2003
`the commencement of section 7A` MEANS `the day` 15 12 2010
`the commencement of section 32B` MEANS `the day` 3 6 2022

-- Rounding a length of time up to whole days ("each day during which the false imprisonment continues", s 32E(1)(c)).
GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`whole days in` x MEANS
    IF FLOOR x EQUALS x
        THEN x
        ELSE FLOOR x PLUS 1

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `s 4 -- does this Act apply to my claim`

-- s 4(1): "This Act, other than Part 4A, applies in relation to all civil claims for damages for personal injuries, other
-- than those excluded by virtue of this section, whether the claims arise under the common law or a statute." s 4(2):
-- "This Act, other than Part 4, Division 6 and Part 4A, applies only in relation to a personal injury alleged to have been
-- caused by an incident that occurs after the commencement of this Act." s 4(3): the excluded claims, from all but
-- Part 4, Division 6. s 4(5): "dust-related condition means" the listed diseases or "any other pathological condition of
-- the lungs, pleura or peritoneum that is attributable to dust". s 17B(5): "Despite section 4(2)", Part 3A Divisions 1,
-- 4, 5 and 6 apply to a cause of action that arose before or after the commencement of Part 3A.
-- s 4(4) lets the Regulations exclude further claims; none are deposited (NOTES.md).
DECLARE `The kind of claim` IS ONE OF
    `a claim for benefits for a death or injury from a motor accident under the Motor Accidents (Compensation) Act 1979`
    `a claim for compensation under the Return to Work Act 1986`
    `a claim about goods under section 106, 107, 118, 127 or 136 of the Australian Consumer Law, for a personal injury`
    `a claim for damages for a personal injury (for example in negligence, or for breach of contract or of a statutory duty)`

DECLARE `What my injury is` IS ONE OF
    `a disease named in section 4(5)(a) (aluminosis, asbestosis, asbestos induced carcinoma, asbestos related pleural disease, bagassosis, berylliosis, byssinosis, coal dust pneumoconiosis, farmer's lung, hard metal pneumoconiosis, mesothelioma, silicosis, silico-tuberculosis or talcosis)`
    `another condition of the lungs, pleura or peritoneum that is attributable to dust`
    `some other injury (including a psychological injury, a disease not caused by dust, or a death)`

DECLARE `Does this Act apply` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Act applies to my claim (s 4(1)); Part 4A applies only to claims by a person in custody for assault, battery or false imprisonment`
    `mostly no: my claim is excluded from the Act by section 4(3)(a) (motor accident benefits); only Part 4, Division 6 (structured settlements) applies`
    `mostly no: my claim is excluded from the Act by section 4(3)(b) (workers compensation); only Part 4, Division 6 (structured settlements) applies`
    `mostly no: my claim is excluded from the Act by section 4(3)(c) (a dust-related condition); only Part 4, Division 6 (structured settlements) applies`
    `mostly no: my claim is excluded from the Act by section 4(3)(e) (Australian Consumer Law goods claim); only Part 4, Division 6 (structured settlements) applies`
    `mostly no: the incident happened before the Act commenced on 1 May 2003 (s 4(2)); only Part 4, Division 6 applies, and for a child abuse claim Part 3A Divisions 1, 4, 5 and 6 (s 17B(5))`

@export I was injured in the Northern Territory. Does the Personal Injuries (Liabilities and Damages) Act 2003 apply to my claim?
GIVEN `the kind of claim` IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim I am making for the injury
      `what my injury is` IS A `What my injury is` @desc What the injury is, so far as it matters for the dust-disease exclusion
      `the date of the incident` IS A DATE @desc The date of the incident, accident or act alleged to have caused the injury (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Does this Act apply`
DECIDE `Does this Act apply question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the kind of claim` EQUALS `a claim for benefits for a death or injury from a motor accident under the Motor Accidents (Compensation) Act 1979`
            THEN `mostly no: my claim is excluded from the Act by section 4(3)(a) (motor accident benefits); only Part 4, Division 6 (structured settlements) applies`
        IF `the kind of claim` EQUALS `a claim for compensation under the Return to Work Act 1986`
            THEN `mostly no: my claim is excluded from the Act by section 4(3)(b) (workers compensation); only Part 4, Division 6 (structured settlements) applies`
        IF `the kind of claim` EQUALS `a claim about goods under section 106, 107, 118, 127 or 136 of the Australian Consumer Law, for a personal injury`
            THEN `mostly no: my claim is excluded from the Act by section 4(3)(e) (Australian Consumer Law goods claim); only Part 4, Division 6 (structured settlements) applies`
        IF (NOT (`what my injury is` EQUALS `some other injury (including a psychological injury, a disease not caused by dust, or a death)`))
            THEN `mostly no: my claim is excluded from the Act by section 4(3)(c) (a dust-related condition); only Part 4, Division 6 (structured settlements) applies`
        IF `the date of the incident` `falls before` `the commencement of this Act`
            THEN `mostly no: the incident happened before the Act commenced on 1 May 2003 (s 4(2)); only Part 4, Division 6 applies, and for a child abuse claim Part 3A Divisions 1, 4, 5 and 6 (s 17B(5))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the Act applies to my claim (s 4(1)); Part 4A applies only to claims by a person in custody for assault, battery or false imprisonment`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `s 5 -- a death: does Part 4 apply to a Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act claim (arises only if someone has died)`

-- s 5(1): "This Act does not limit the operation of the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974." s 5(2): "If an action is
-- brought under the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 in relation to a person's death: (a) that occurs after the
-- commencement of this Act; and (b) that is caused by a negligent act, Part 4 of this Act applies in addition to, and not
-- in derogation of, section 10 of the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 in relation to the assessment of damages".
DECLARE `Does Part 4 apply to a death claim` IS ONE OF
    `yes: Part 4 (the limits on damages) applies to the assessment, in addition to section 10 of the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (s 5(2))`
    `no: section 5(2) does not apply Part 4 to this death; this Act does not limit the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (s 5(1))`

@export Someone in my family died in the Northern Territory and we are claiming under the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974. Do the damages limits in Part 4 of the Personal Injuries (Liabilities and Damages) Act apply?
GIVEN `the date of the death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person died (ISO 8601)
      `the death was caused by a negligent act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused by a negligent act (rather than, for example, an intentional one)
GIVETH A `Does Part 4 apply to a death claim`
DECIDE `Does Part 4 apply to a death claim question` IS
    IF `the death was caused by a negligent act` AND `the date of the death` `falls on or after` `the commencement of this Act`
        THEN `yes: Part 4 (the limits on damages) applies to the assessment, in addition to section 10 of the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (s 5(2))`
        ELSE `no: section 5(2) does not apply Part 4 to this death; this Act does not limit the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (s 5(1))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `ss 7, 7A, 8, 9, 10 -- can the person who hurt me escape liability`

-- s 7(1): "A volunteer does not incur personal civil liability for a personal injury caused by an act done in good faith
-- and without recklessness while doing community work for a community organisation." (2): not if the volunteer "(a) knew,
-- or ought reasonably to have known, that he or she was acting outside the scope of his or her authority or contrary to
-- the instructions of the community organisation; or (b) did the act while intoxicated." (3): the organisation incurs the
-- liability "as if the volunteer were an employee". (4): for an Agency or department, the Territory. (6): management
-- committee members are not personally liable. (7): "community organisation means a religious body, a body corporate, or
-- an Agency or department of the Territory"; "community work (a) means work done for" the listed purposes "(viii) any
-- purpose prescribed by regulation; but (b) does not include work done under" a community work or correction order;
-- "volunteer ... means a person doing community work for that organisation: (a) who receives no remuneration ... other
-- than (i) remuneration that the person would receive whether or not he or she did that work; or (ii) the reimbursement
-- of reasonable expenses ...; or (b) who receives remuneration that does not exceed the amount, if any, prescribed".
DECLARE `The kind of organisation` IS ONE OF
    `a religious body`
    `a body corporate (for example an incorporated association or a company)`
    `an Agency or department of the Territory`
    `some other body (for example an unincorporated club or group)`

DECLARE `The purpose of the work` IS ONE OF
    `a purpose listed in section 7(7)(a)(i) to (vii) (religious, educational, charitable, benevolent, literature, science, the arts, sport, recreation, amusement, the environment, a community, social or cultural centre, a local community's interests, or a political purpose)`
    `some other purpose`

DECLARE `Who is liable for the volunteer` IS ONE OF
    `the volunteer is not personally liable; the organisation is liable as if the volunteer were its employee, and members of its management committee are not personally liable (s 7(1), (3), (6))`
    `the volunteer is not personally liable; the Territory is liable as if the volunteer were the Agency's employee (s 7(1), (3), (4))`
    `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: the organisation is not a community organisation (a religious body, a body corporate, or a Territory Agency or department) (s 7(7))`
    `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: work done under a community work or correction order is not community work (s 7(7))`
    `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: they were paid for the work (s 7(7))`
    `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: the act was not done in good faith and without recklessness (s 7(1))`
    `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: they knew or ought reasonably to have known they were acting outside their authority or against the organisation's instructions (s 7(2)(a))`
    `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: they did the act while intoxicated (s 7(2)(b))`

@export I was injured by a volunteer doing community work in the Northern Territory. Is the volunteer personally liable, or must I claim against the organisation?
GIVEN `the organisation` IS A `The kind of organisation` @desc What kind of body organised, directed or supervised the volunteer's work
      `the purpose of the work` IS A `The purpose of the work` @desc What the work was being done for
      `the work was under a court or correction order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the volunteer was doing the work under a community work order (Fines and Penalties (Recovery) Act 2001, Youth Justice Act 2005 or the former Sentencing Act provision) or a community correction or intensive community correction order
      `the volunteer was paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the volunteer was paid for the work, beyond pay they would have got anyway and reimbursement of reasonable expenses
      `the act was in good faith and without recklessness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the volunteer acted in good faith and without recklessness
      `they knew they were acting outside their authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the volunteer knew, or ought reasonably to have known, they were acting outside their authority or against the organisation's instructions
      `the volunteer was intoxicated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the volunteer was under the influence of alcohol or a drug so that their capacity to exercise proper care and skill was significantly impaired
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Who is liable for the volunteer`
DECIDE `Who is liable for the volunteer question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the organisation` EQUALS `some other body (for example an unincorporated club or group)`
            THEN `answered` `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: the organisation is not a community organisation (a religious body, a body corporate, or a Territory Agency or department) (s 7(7))`
        IF `the work was under a court or correction order`
            THEN `answered` `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: work done under a community work or correction order is not community work (s 7(7))`
        IF `the purpose of the work` EQUALS `some other purpose`
            THEN `not covered` `"community work" includes work for a purpose prescribed by regulation (s 7(7)(a)(viii)); the Regulations are not deposited`
        IF `the volunteer was paid`
            THEN `not covered` `"volunteer" includes a person paid no more than an amount prescribed by the Regulations (s 7(7)(b)); the Regulations are not deposited`
        IF (NOT `the act was in good faith and without recklessness`)
            THEN `answered` `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: the act was not done in good faith and without recklessness (s 7(1))`
        IF `they knew they were acting outside their authority`
            THEN `answered` `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: they knew or ought reasonably to have known they were acting outside their authority or against the organisation's instructions (s 7(2)(a))`
        IF `the volunteer was intoxicated`
            THEN `answered` `section 7 does not protect the volunteer: they did the act while intoxicated (s 7(2)(b))`
        IF `the organisation` EQUALS `an Agency or department of the Territory`
            THEN `answered` `the volunteer is not personally liable; the Territory is liable as if the volunteer were the Agency's employee (s 7(1), (3), (4))`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `the volunteer is not personally liable; the organisation is liable as if the volunteer were its employee, and members of its management committee are not personally liable (s 7(1), (3), (6))`

-- s 7A(1): a donor "does not incur civil liability for a personal injury caused by the consumption of the food or use of
-- the grocery product" in the circumstances of (2): (a) donated "(i) in good faith for a charitable or benevolent purpose;
-- and (ii) with the intention that the consumer ... would not have to pay for it"; (b) fit or safe when it left the donor;
-- (c) if it needed particular handling, the donor told the recipient; (d) if it stayed fit or safe only for a limited time,
-- the donor told the recipient. (4): "grocery product means" a personal hygiene product, a household cleaning product, a
-- non-prescription medical product, or "another product prescribed by regulation"; "person who donates ... does not
-- include a person who distributes food or a grocery product donated by another person". s 36: s 7A "applies only in
-- relation to a person who donates ... after the commencement" of the Amendment Act 2010.
DECLARE `What was donated` IS ONE OF
    `food`
    `a personal hygiene product`
    `a household cleaning product`
    `a medical product sold without a prescription`
    `some other product`

DECLARE `Is the donor liable` IS ONE OF
    `the donor is not liable for the injury (s 7A(1), (2))`
    `section 7A gives no protection: a person who only distributes food or products donated by someone else is not a donor (s 7A(4))`
    `section 7A gives no protection: the donation was made before section 7A commenced on 15 December 2010 (s 36)`
    `section 7A gives no protection: the donation was not in good faith for a charitable or benevolent purpose, free to the consumer (s 7A(2)(a))`
    `section 7A gives no protection: the food was not fit to eat, or the product not safe to use, when it left the donor (s 7A(2)(b))`
    `section 7A gives no protection: the donor did not pass on the handling requirements (s 7A(2)(c))`
    `section 7A gives no protection: the donor did not pass on the time limit (s 7A(2)(d))`

@export I was made ill or hurt by donated food or a donated grocery product in the Northern Territory. Can I hold the donor liable?
GIVEN `what was donated` IS A `What was donated` @desc What was donated
      `the date of the donation` IS A DATE @desc The date the donor donated it (ISO 8601)
      `the donor only passed on someone else's donation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person I would claim against only distributed food or products that someone else had donated
      `it was donated in good faith for a charitable purpose, free to the consumer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was donated in good faith for a charitable or benevolent purpose, intending that the person using it would not have to pay
      `it was fit or safe when it left the donor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the food was fit to eat (not unsafe or unsuitable under the Food Act 2004), or the product safe to use, when it left the donor's possession or control
      `it needed particular handling` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it needed to be handled in a particular way (for example kept refrigerated) to stay fit or safe
      `the donor passed on the handling requirements` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only if it needed particular handling: whether the donor told the person they gave it to how it must be handled
      `it stayed fit or safe only for a limited time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it stayed fit or safe for only a limited time after leaving the donor (for example a use-by date)
      `the donor passed on the time limit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only if it had a limited life: whether the donor told the person they gave it to of that time limit
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Is the donor liable`
DECIDE `Is the donor liable question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `what was donated` EQUALS `some other product`
            THEN `not covered` `"grocery product" includes a product prescribed by regulation (s 7A(4)(d)); the Regulations are not deposited`
        IF `the donor only passed on someone else's donation`
            THEN `answered` `section 7A gives no protection: a person who only distributes food or products donated by someone else is not a donor (s 7A(4))`
        IF `the date of the donation` `falls before` `the commencement of section 7A`
            THEN `answered` `section 7A gives no protection: the donation was made before section 7A commenced on 15 December 2010 (s 36)`
        IF (NOT `it was donated in good faith for a charitable purpose, free to the consumer`)
            THEN `answered` `section 7A gives no protection: the donation was not in good faith for a charitable or benevolent purpose, free to the consumer (s 7A(2)(a))`
        IF (NOT `it was fit or safe when it left the donor`)
            THEN `answered` `section 7A gives no protection: the food was not fit to eat, or the product not safe to use, when it left the donor (s 7A(2)(b))`
        IF `it needed particular handling` AND (NOT `the donor passed on the handling requirements`)
            THEN `answered` `section 7A gives no protection: the donor did not pass on the handling requirements (s 7A(2)(c))`
        IF `it stayed fit or safe only for a limited time` AND (NOT `the donor passed on the time limit`)
            THEN `answered` `section 7A gives no protection: the donor did not pass on the time limit (s 7A(2)(d))`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `the donor is not liable for the injury (s 7A(1), (2))`

-- s 8(1): "A good Samaritan does not incur personal civil liability for a personal injury caused by an act done in good
-- faith and without recklessness while giving emergency assistance to a person." (2): the same for a good Samaritan with
-- medical qualifications giving advice about treatment. (3): "This section does not apply if the good Samaritan was
-- intoxicated". (4): "good Samaritan means (a) a person who, acting without expectation of payment or other
-- consideration, comes to the aid of a person who is apparently in need of emergency assistance; or (b) a person with
-- medical qualifications who, acting without expectation of payment ..., gives advice about the treatment of a person who
-- is apparently in need of emergency medical assistance."
DECLARE `What the helper did` IS ONE OF
    `came to my aid in an emergency`
    `gave advice about my treatment in a medical emergency, as a person with medical qualifications (a doctor, a health professional recognised by statute, or an ambulance officer or paramedic)`

DECLARE `Is the good Samaritan liable` IS ONE OF
    `the helper is not personally liable for an act done while giving me emergency assistance (s 8(1))`
    `the helper is not personally liable for the advice about my treatment (s 8(2))`
    `section 8 gives no protection: the helper expected payment or other consideration, so is not a good Samaritan (s 8(4))`
    `section 8 gives no protection: I was not apparently in need of emergency assistance, so the helper is not a good Samaritan (s 8(4))`
    `section 8 gives no protection: the helper did not act in good faith and without recklessness (s 8(1), (2))`
    `section 8 gives no protection: the helper was intoxicated (s 8(3))`

@export Someone who stopped to help me in an emergency in the Northern Territory injured me, or gave advice that harmed me. Can I hold them liable?
GIVEN `what the helper did` IS A `What the helper did` @desc What the person who helped did
      `the helper expected payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the helper acted expecting payment or other consideration (for example on duty, or for a fee)
      `I was apparently in need of emergency help` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I was apparently in need of emergency assistance (my life or safety endangered in an emergency), or of emergency medical assistance for advice
      `the helper acted in good faith and without recklessness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the helper acted in good faith and without recklessness
      `the helper was intoxicated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the helper was under the influence of alcohol or a drug so that their capacity to exercise proper care and skill was significantly impaired
GIVETH A `Is the good Samaritan liable`
DECIDE `Is the good Samaritan liable question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the helper expected payment`
            THEN `section 8 gives no protection: the helper expected payment or other consideration, so is not a good Samaritan (s 8(4))`
        IF (NOT `I was apparently in need of emergency help`)
            THEN `section 8 gives no protection: I was not apparently in need of emergency assistance, so the helper is not a good Samaritan (s 8(4))`
        IF `the helper was intoxicated`
            THEN `section 8 gives no protection: the helper was intoxicated (s 8(3))`
        IF (NOT `the helper acted in good faith and without recklessness`)
            THEN `section 8 gives no protection: the helper did not act in good faith and without recklessness (s 8(1), (2))`
        IF `what the helper did` EQUALS `came to my aid in an emergency`
            THEN `the helper is not personally liable for an act done while giving me emergency assistance (s 8(1))`
        OTHERWISE `the helper is not personally liable for the advice about my treatment (s 8(2))`

-- s 9(1): "The occupier or owner of premises does not incur civil liability for a personal injury to a person who: (a) is
-- entering or has entered the premises; and (b) has the intention of committing, is committing or has committed on those
-- premises an offence punishable by imprisonment." (2): "occupier means a person occupying or having control of the
-- premises."
DECLARE `Can I claim against the occupier` IS ONE OF
    `no: the occupier or owner is not liable for an injury to a person entering premises who intends to commit, is committing or has committed an imprisonable offence there (s 9(1))`
    `section 9 does not stand in the way of my claim`

@export I was injured on someone's premises in the Northern Territory. Does section 9 stop me claiming against the occupier or owner because of an offence?
GIVEN `I claim against the occupier or owner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person I claim against is the owner of the premises, or occupies or has control of them
      `I was entering or had entered the premises` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I was injured when entering, or after entering, the premises
      `I meant to commit, was committing or had committed an imprisonable offence there` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, on those premises, I intended to commit, was committing or had committed an offence punishable by imprisonment
GIVETH A `Can I claim against the occupier`
DECIDE `Can I claim against the occupier question` IS
    IF `I claim against the occupier or owner` AND `I was entering or had entered the premises` AND `I meant to commit, was committing or had committed an imprisonable offence there`
        THEN `no: the occupier or owner is not liable for an injury to a person entering premises who intends to commit, is committing or has committed an imprisonable offence there (s 9(1))`
        ELSE `section 9 does not stand in the way of my claim`

-- s 10(1): "A person does not incur civil liability for a personal injury if the court is satisfied on the balance of
-- probabilities: (a) that the injury occurred while the injured person was engaged in conduct constituting an offence
-- punishable by imprisonment; and (b) that the injured person's conduct contributed materially to the risk of that
-- injury." (2): "Subsection (1) does not apply if the court is satisfied: (a) that the circumstances of the particular case
-- are exceptional; and (b) that to exclude liability in the circumstances of that particular case would be harsh and
-- unjust."
DECLARE `Does my own offence bar my claim` IS ONE OF
    `yes: no one is liable for my injury, because I was hurt while committing an imprisonable offence that contributed materially to the risk (s 10(1))`
    `no: the court is satisfied the case is exceptional and excluding liability would be harsh and unjust, so section 10(1) does not apply (s 10(2))`
    `no: section 10 does not stand in the way of my claim`

@export I was injured in the Northern Territory while doing something that may have been a criminal offence. Does that stop me claiming?
GIVEN `the court is satisfied I was hurt while committing an imprisonable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that I was injured while engaged in conduct that is an offence punishable by imprisonment
      `the court is satisfied my conduct contributed materially to the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that my conduct contributed materially to the risk of the injury
      `the court is satisfied the case is exceptional` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that the circumstances of my case are exceptional
      `the court is satisfied excluding liability would be harsh and unjust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that excluding liability in my case would be harsh and unjust
GIVETH A `Does my own offence bar my claim`
DECIDE `Does my own offence bar my claim question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`the court is satisfied I was hurt while committing an imprisonable offence` AND `the court is satisfied my conduct contributed materially to the risk`))
            THEN `no: section 10 does not stand in the way of my claim`
        IF `the court is satisfied the case is exceptional` AND `the court is satisfied excluding liability would be harsh and unjust`
            THEN `no: the court is satisfied the case is exceptional and excluding liability would be harsh and unjust, so section 10(1) does not apply (s 10(2))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: no one is liable for my injury, because I was hurt while committing an imprisonable offence that contributed materially to the risk (s 10(1))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `ss 12, 13 -- can I use their apology as evidence (arises only if someone apologised)`

-- s 12: "An expression of regret is an oral or written statement by a person: (a) that expresses regret for an incident
-- that is alleged to have caused a personal injury; and (b) that does not contain an acknowledgement of fault by that
-- person." s 13: "An expression of regret about a personal injury made at any time before the commencement of a
-- proceeding in respect of that injury is not admissible as evidence in that proceeding." s 35(2): s 13 applies whenever
-- the expression was made.
DECLARE `Can I use the apology` IS ONE OF
    `no: an expression of regret made before the proceeding began is not admissible as evidence in it (s 13)`
    `section 13 does not keep it out: the statement acknowledged fault, so it is not an expression of regret (s 12(b)); whether it is admissible is left to the law of evidence`
    `section 13 does not keep it out: it was made after the proceeding began; whether it is admissible is left to the law of evidence`
    `section 13 does not keep it out: the statement did not express regret for the incident (s 12(a)); whether it is admissible is left to the law of evidence`

@export The person I am claiming against in the Northern Territory said sorry. Can their apology be used as evidence in my case?
GIVEN `the statement expressed regret for the incident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what they said or wrote expressed regret for the incident that injured me
      `the statement acknowledged their fault` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement also contained an acknowledgement of fault by them (for example "it was my fault")
      `it was made before the proceeding began` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement was made before my court proceeding for the injury was commenced
GIVETH A `Can I use the apology`
DECIDE `Can I use the apology question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `the statement expressed regret for the incident`)
            THEN `section 13 does not keep it out: the statement did not express regret for the incident (s 12(a)); whether it is admissible is left to the law of evidence`
        IF `the statement acknowledged their fault`
            THEN `section 13 does not keep it out: the statement acknowledged fault, so it is not an expression of regret (s 12(b)); whether it is admissible is left to the law of evidence`
        IF (NOT `it was made before the proceeding began`)
            THEN `section 13 does not keep it out: it was made after the proceeding began; whether it is admissible is left to the law of evidence`
        OTHERWISE `no: an expression of regret made before the proceeding began is not admissible as evidence in it (s 13)`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `ss 14-17 -- will my damages be reduced for contributory negligence because of alcohol or drugs`

-- s 3: "intoxicated means under the influence of alcohol or a drug to the extent that the capacity to exercise proper
-- care and skill is significantly impaired."
-- s 14(1): a presumption of contributory negligence "if: (a) the injured person was intoxicated at the time of the
-- incident ...; and (b) the respondent alleges contributory negligence." (2): "rebutted if the claimant establishes on the
-- balance of probabilities that the injured person's intoxication: (a) did not materially contribute to the incident; or
-- (b) was involuntary."
-- s 15(1): a presumption "if: (a) at the time of the incident ..., the injured person: (i) had attained 16 years of age;
-- (ii) relied on the care and skill of another person who was intoxicated; and (iii) was aware, or ought to have been
-- aware, that the other person was intoxicated; (b) the injury was caused by the negligence of the other person; and (c)
-- the respondent alleges the contributory negligence of the injured person." (2): "rebutted only if the claimant
-- establishes on the balance of probabilities: (a) that the other person's intoxication did not materially contribute to
-- the incident; or (b) that the injured person could not reasonably be expected to have avoided the risk".
-- s 16: a court finding of 0.08 grams or more of alcohol in 210 litres of breath, or in 100 millilitres of blood, is
-- "conclusive evidence ... that the person was intoxicated at the time of the incident".
-- s 17: "If contributory negligence is established under this Division, the court must assess damages on the basis that
-- the damages ... are to be reduced ... by 25% or a greater percentage determined by the court".
-- Part 3 has no Divisions; "this Division" in ss 16 and 17 is read as Part 3 (NOTES.md, finding 3).

-- A person is intoxicated if they were (s 3), or conclusively if a court finds a reading of 0.08 or more (s 16).
GIVEN `the person was intoxicated` IS A BOOLEAN, `the person's alcohol reading was 0.08 or more` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `intoxicated under sections 3 and 16` IF `the person was intoxicated` OR `the person's alcohol reading was 0.08 or more`

DECLARE `Is contributory negligence presumed` IS ONE OF
    `no presumption: the respondent does not allege contributory negligence (ss 14(1)(b), 15(1)(c))`
    `no presumption: neither section 14 nor section 15 applies on these facts`
    `a presumption arises but I rebut it, so Part 3 does not establish contributory negligence (ss 14(2), 15(2)); the ordinary law of contributory negligence still applies`
    `yes: contributory negligence is presumed and not rebutted, so it is established and my damages are reduced by at least 25% (ss 14, 15, 17)`

@export I was injured in the Northern Territory when I had been drinking or taking drugs, or when I relied on someone who had. Is contributory negligence presumed against me?
GIVEN `the respondent alleges contributory negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person I claim against alleges that I was contributorily negligent
      `I was intoxicated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether at the time of the incident I was under the influence of alcohol or a drug so that my capacity to exercise proper care and skill was significantly impaired
      `my alcohol reading was 0.08 or more` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that at or about the time of the incident I had 0.08 grams or more of alcohol in 210 litres of breath or in 100 millilitres of blood
      `I prove my intoxication did not contribute or was involuntary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I establish, on the balance of probabilities, that my intoxication did not materially contribute to the incident, or was involuntary (for example my drink was spiked)
      `my age at the time` IS A NUMBER @desc My age in whole years at the time of the incident
      `I relied on another person's care and skill` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I relied on the care and skill of another person (for example the driver of the car I was in)
      `that person was intoxicated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that person was under the influence of alcohol or a drug so that their capacity to exercise proper care and skill was significantly impaired
      `their alcohol reading was 0.08 or more` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that the other person had 0.08 grams or more of alcohol in 210 litres of breath or in 100 millilitres of blood at or about the time
      `I knew or ought to have known they were intoxicated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I was aware, or ought to have been aware, that the other person was intoxicated
      `their negligence caused my injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether my injury was caused by that other person's negligence
      `I prove their intoxication did not contribute or I could not have avoided the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I establish, on the balance of probabilities, that their intoxication did not materially contribute to the incident, or that I could not reasonably be expected to have avoided the risk
GIVETH A `Is contributory negligence presumed`
DECIDE `Is contributory negligence presumed question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `the respondent alleges contributory negligence`)
            THEN `no presumption: the respondent does not allege contributory negligence (ss 14(1)(b), 15(1)(c))`
        IF (NOT (`section 14 presumes it` OR `section 15 presumes it`))
            THEN `no presumption: neither section 14 nor section 15 applies on these facts`
        IF (`section 14 presumes it` AND (NOT `I prove my intoxication did not contribute or was involuntary`)) OR (`section 15 presumes it` AND (NOT `I prove their intoxication did not contribute or I could not have avoided the risk`))
            THEN `yes: contributory negligence is presumed and not rebutted, so it is established and my damages are reduced by at least 25% (ss 14, 15, 17)`
        OTHERWISE `a presumption arises but I rebut it, so Part 3 does not establish contributory negligence (ss 14(2), 15(2)); the ordinary law of contributory negligence still applies`
    WHERE
        `section 14 presumes it` MEANS `intoxicated under sections 3 and 16` `I was intoxicated` `my alcohol reading was 0.08 or more`
        `section 15 presumes it` MEANS
                `my age at the time` AT LEAST 16
            AND `I relied on another person's care and skill`
            AND `intoxicated under sections 3 and 16` `that person was intoxicated` `their alcohol reading was 0.08 or more`
            AND `I knew or ought to have known they were intoxicated`
            AND `their negligence caused my injury`

-- s 17: the reduction. The court's percentage is an input; below 25 it cannot go.
@export Contributory negligence has been established against me under Part 3 of the Northern Territory Personal Injuries (Liabilities and Damages) Act. How much are my damages reduced?
GIVEN `my damages without contributory negligence` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages I would get if there were no contributory negligence, in dollars
      `the percentage the court determines` IS A NUMBER @desc The reduction the court determines to be appropriate, as a percentage (for example 30 for 30%); the Act's minimum is 25
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `My damages after the section 17 reduction question` IS
    `my damages without contributory negligence` MINUS ((`my damages without contributory negligence` TIMES `the reduction`) DIVIDED BY 100)
    WHERE
        `the reduction` MEANS min 100 (max 25 `the percentage the court determines`)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Part 3A -- child abuse in an institution (arises only if I was abused as a child)`

-- s 3: "child means an individual who is under the age of 18 years." "child abuse means any of the following perpetrated
-- against a child: (a) sexual abuse; (b) serious physical abuse; (c) psychological abuse that arises from abuse specified
-- in paragraph (a) or (b) or both." s 3(2): "For subsection (1), definition child abuse, paragraph (b), conduct that was
-- lawful at the time it occurred is not child abuse."
DECLARE `What was done to me` IS ONE OF
    `sexual abuse`
    `serious physical abuse`
    `psychological abuse arising from sexual abuse`
    `psychological abuse arising from serious physical abuse`
    `psychological abuse alone`
    `something else (for example neglect)`

DECLARE `Is it child abuse` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is child abuse under the Act (s 3)`
    `no: child abuse must be perpetrated against a child, an individual under 18 (s 3)`
    `no: physical conduct that was lawful at the time it occurred is not child abuse (s 3(2))`
    `no: child abuse means sexual abuse, serious physical abuse, or psychological abuse arising from either (s 3)`

@export I was abused as a child in the Northern Territory. Is what happened to me "child abuse" under the Personal Injuries (Liabilities and Damages) Act?
GIVEN `what was done to me` IS A `What was done to me` @desc What kind of abuse was perpetrated against me
      `my age at the time` IS A NUMBER @desc My age in whole years when it happened
      `the conduct was lawful at the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for serious physical abuse (or psychological abuse arising from it): whether the physical conduct was lawful at the time it occurred (for example corporal punishment then permitted)
GIVETH A `Is it child abuse`
DECIDE `Is it child abuse question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `my age at the time` AT LEAST 18
            THEN `no: child abuse must be perpetrated against a child, an individual under 18 (s 3)`
        IF `what was done to me` EQUALS `psychological abuse alone` OR `what was done to me` EQUALS `something else (for example neglect)`
            THEN `no: child abuse means sexual abuse, serious physical abuse, or psychological abuse arising from either (s 3)`
        IF (`what was done to me` EQUALS `serious physical abuse` OR `what was done to me` EQUALS `psychological abuse arising from serious physical abuse`) AND `the conduct was lawful at the time`
            THEN `no: physical conduct that was lawful at the time it occurred is not child abuse (s 3(2))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: it is child abuse under the Act (s 3)`

-- s 17B(1): "This Part applies to the following institutions while exercising care, supervision or authority over a
-- child: (a) a body; (b) an entity; (c) a body corporate; (d) a group of persons; (e) an association; (f) an
-- organisation." (2): "An institution does not include a family or an individual." (3), (4): incorporated or not; no
-- written constitution, fixed membership or prescribed attribute needed.
DECLARE `What the organisation is` IS ONE OF
    `a body, entity, body corporate, group of persons, association or organisation (incorporated or not, with or without a written constitution or fixed membership)`
    `a family`
    `an individual`

DECLARE `Is it an institution under Part 3A` IS ONE OF
    `yes: Part 3A applies to it as an institution exercising care, supervision or authority over me (s 17B)`
    `no: an institution does not include a family or an individual (s 17B(2))`
    `no: Part 3A applies to an institution only while it is exercising care, supervision or authority over the child (s 17B(1))`

@export I was abused as a child in the Northern Territory while in the care of an organisation. Is it an "institution" that Part 3A of the Personal Injuries (Liabilities and Damages) Act applies to?
GIVEN `what the organisation is` IS A `What the organisation is` @desc What kind of body or person had care of me
      `it had care, supervision or authority over me at the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was exercising care, supervision or authority over me when the abuse happened
GIVETH A `Is it an institution under Part 3A`
DECIDE `Is it an institution question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`what the organisation is` EQUALS `a body, entity, body corporate, group of persons, association or organisation (incorporated or not, with or without a written constitution or fixed membership)`))
            THEN `no: an institution does not include a family or an individual (s 17B(2))`
        IF (NOT `it had care, supervision or authority over me at the time`)
            THEN `no: Part 3A applies to an institution only while it is exercising care, supervision or authority over the child (s 17B(1))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: Part 3A applies to it as an institution exercising care, supervision or authority over me (s 17B)`

-- s 17C(1): "An individual associated with an institution includes: (a) an office holder, officer, owner, trustee,
-- employee, volunteer or contractor of the institution; and (b) if the institution is a religious institution -- a
-- religious leader or a member of the religious institution; and (c) if the institution by any arrangement, contract or
-- delegation made another institution responsible for exercising care, supervision or authority over a child -- an
-- individual specified in paragraph (a) or (b) in relation to that other institution; and (d) if the institution by any
-- arrangement, contract or delegation made an individual responsible ... -- that individual; and (e) any other prescribed
-- individual." (2): "an individual is not an individual associated with an institution solely because the institution
-- wholly or partly funds or regulates another institution that the individual is associated with."
DECLARE `The abuser's connection` IS ONE OF
    `an office holder, officer, owner, trustee, employee, volunteer or contractor of the institution`
    `a religious leader or member of the institution, which is a religious institution`
    `one of those people in another institution that the institution made responsible for my care, supervision or authority`
    `an individual the institution made responsible for my care, supervision or authority by an arrangement, contract or delegation`
    `associated only with another institution that this institution funds or regulates`
    `some other connection`

DECLARE `Is the abuser associated with the institution` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the abuser is an individual associated with the institution (s 17C(1)(a))`
    `yes: the abuser is an individual associated with the institution (s 17C(1)(b))`
    `yes: the abuser is an individual associated with the institution (s 17C(1)(c))`
    `yes: the abuser is an individual associated with the institution (s 17C(1)(d))`
    `no: an individual is not associated with an institution only because it funds or regulates another institution they are associated with (s 17C(2))`

@export I was abused as a child in the Northern Territory. Was the abuser an "individual associated with" the institution that had care of me?
GIVEN `the abuser's connection` IS A `The abuser's connection` @desc How the person who abused me was connected with the institution
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Is the abuser associated with the institution`
DECIDE `Is the abuser associated question` IS
    CONSIDER `the abuser's connection`
    WHEN `an office holder, officer, owner, trustee, employee, volunteer or contractor of the institution` THEN `answered` `yes: the abuser is an individual associated with the institution (s 17C(1)(a))`
    WHEN `a religious leader or member of the institution, which is a religious institution` THEN `answered` `yes: the abuser is an individual associated with the institution (s 17C(1)(b))`
    WHEN `one of those people in another institution that the institution made responsible for my care, supervision or authority` THEN `answered` `yes: the abuser is an individual associated with the institution (s 17C(1)(c))`
    WHEN `an individual the institution made responsible for my care, supervision or authority by an arrangement, contract or delegation` THEN `answered` `yes: the abuser is an individual associated with the institution (s 17C(1)(d))`
    WHEN `associated only with another institution that this institution funds or regulates` THEN `answered` `no: an individual is not associated with an institution only because it funds or regulates another institution they are associated with (s 17C(2))`
    WHEN `some other connection` THEN `not covered` `"individual associated with an institution" is an "includes" list, and further individuals may be prescribed (s 17C(1)(e)); the Regulations are not deposited and the Act does not say whether others count`

-- s 17D: the duty "to take all reasonable steps to prevent child abuse" by (1) an individual associated with the
-- institution, (2) another child in its care, "while the child is under the care, supervision or authority of the
-- institution". s 17E(2): "If the plaintiff proves on the balance of probabilities an individual associated with the
-- institution perpetrated the child abuse while the individual abused was under the care, supervision or authority of the
-- institution, the institution is taken to have breached the duty of care under section 17D(1), unless the institution
-- proves on the balance of probabilities that it took all reasonable steps to prevent the child abuse." s 17F(2): the same
-- for abuse by another child in its care, and the duty under s 17D(2); s 17F(5): that child "must not be named in any
-- pleadings" and the court "must be closed to the public while the evidence is given". ss 17E(4), 17F(4): against two or
-- more institutions, each equally. s 4(2): the Act (Part 3A Divisions 2 and 3 included, since s 17B(5) does not name them)
-- applies only to an injury from an incident after 1 May 2003 (NOTES.md, finding 6).
DECLARE `Who abused me` IS ONE OF
    `an individual associated with the institution`
    `another child in the institution's care, supervision or authority`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `Is the institution taken to have breached its duty` IS ONE OF
    `yes, unless the institution proves on the balance of probabilities that it took all reasonable steps to prevent the abuse: once I prove the abuse by an individual associated with it while I was in its care, it is taken to have breached its duty under section 17D(1) (s 17E(2)); any award of vicarious liability under section 17G is taken into account (s 17E(5))`
    `yes, unless the institution proves on the balance of probabilities that it took all reasonable steps to prevent the abuse: once I prove the abuse by another child while we were both in its care, it is taken to have breached its duty under section 17D(2) (s 17F(2)); the other child must not be named in the pleadings, and the court is closed while that child gives evidence (s 17F(5))`
    `no: the reversed onus of sections 17E and 17F covers abuse only while I was under the institution's care, supervision or authority; otherwise the ordinary law of negligence applies`
    `no: the reversed onus of sections 17E and 17F covers abuse by an individual associated with the institution or another child in its care; otherwise the ordinary law of negligence applies`
    `no: the abuse happened before the Act commenced on 1 May 2003, and section 17B(5) does not extend Divisions 2 and 3 of Part 3A to it (s 4(2))`

@export I was abused as a child while in the care of an institution in the Northern Territory. Must the institution prove it took all reasonable steps to prevent it?
GIVEN `who abused me` IS A `Who abused me` @desc Who perpetrated the abuse
      `I was in the institution's care at the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I was under the institution's care, supervision or authority when the abuse happened
      `the date of the abuse` IS A DATE @desc The date the abuse happened (the last date, if it went on over time) (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Is the institution taken to have breached its duty`
DECIDE `Is the institution taken to have breached its duty question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the date of the abuse` `falls before` `the commencement of this Act`
            THEN `no: the abuse happened before the Act commenced on 1 May 2003, and section 17B(5) does not extend Divisions 2 and 3 of Part 3A to it (s 4(2))`
        IF `who abused me` EQUALS `someone else`
            THEN `no: the reversed onus of sections 17E and 17F covers abuse by an individual associated with the institution or another child in its care; otherwise the ordinary law of negligence applies`
        IF (NOT `I was in the institution's care at the time`)
            THEN `no: the reversed onus of sections 17E and 17F covers abuse only while I was under the institution's care, supervision or authority; otherwise the ordinary law of negligence applies`
        IF `who abused me` EQUALS `an individual associated with the institution`
            THEN `yes, unless the institution proves on the balance of probabilities that it took all reasonable steps to prevent the abuse: once I prove the abuse by an individual associated with it while I was in its care, it is taken to have breached its duty under section 17D(1) (s 17E(2)); any award of vicarious liability under section 17G is taken into account (s 17E(5))`
        OTHERWISE `yes, unless the institution proves on the balance of probabilities that it took all reasonable steps to prevent the abuse: once I prove the abuse by another child while we were both in its care, it is taken to have breached its duty under section 17D(2) (s 17F(2)); the other child must not be named in the pleadings, and the court is closed while that child gives evidence (s 17F(5))`

-- s 17G(1): "An institution is vicariously liable for personal injury arising from child abuse perpetrated by an employee
-- of the institution or an individual akin to an employee if: (a) the institution places the employee or individual akin
-- to an employee in a position or situation that gives occasion for child abuse of the child; and (b) the employee or
-- individual akin to an employee uses that occasion to perpetrate child abuse of the child." (2): the court must take into
-- account authority, power or control, trust, and the ability to achieve intimacy (folded into the court's finding, an
-- input). (3): "does not affect, and is in addition to, the law of the Territory with respect to vicarious liability."
-- (5): "individual akin to an employee ... is an individual who: (a) carries out an activity ... that enables the
-- institution to perform its functions, except for an activity: (i) in accordance with a placement arrangement under
-- section 78 of the Care and Protection of Children Act 2007 ...; or (ii) carried out under a contract for services by an
-- individual or corporation that is independent from the institution; and (b) carries out that activity with the
-- intention to benefit the institution."
DECLARE `The abuser's role` IS ONE OF
    `an employee of the institution`
    `someone who carried out activities that enabled the institution to perform its functions, intending to benefit it (for example a volunteer, or a priest or religious leader)`
    `a carer under a placement arrangement under section 78 of the Care and Protection of Children Act 2007 (for example a foster or kinship carer)`
    `an independent contractor working under a contract for services`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `Is the institution vicariously liable` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the institution is vicariously liable for my injury from the abuse (s 17G(1)); any award under section 17E or 17F is taken into account (s 17G(4))`
    `section 17G does not make the institution liable on these facts; the general law of vicarious liability still applies (s 17G(3))`
    `no: the abuse happened before the Act commenced on 1 May 2003, and section 17B(5) does not extend Division 3 of Part 3A to it (s 4(2)); the general law of vicarious liability still applies`

@export I was abused as a child in the Northern Territory by someone working for an institution. Is the institution vicariously liable?
GIVEN `the abuser's role` IS A `The abuser's role` @desc The abuser's role in relation to the institution
      `the court finds the institution placed them in a position that gave occasion for the abuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that the institution placed the abuser in a position or situation that gave occasion for the abuse (taking into account authority, power or control over me, my trust, or the ability to achieve intimacy with me)
      `they used that occasion to abuse me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuser used that occasion to abuse me
      `the date of the abuse` IS A DATE @desc The date the abuse happened (the last date, if it went on over time) (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Is the institution vicariously liable`
DECIDE `Is the institution vicariously liable question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the date of the abuse` `falls before` `the commencement of this Act`
            THEN `no: the abuse happened before the Act commenced on 1 May 2003, and section 17B(5) does not extend Division 3 of Part 3A to it (s 4(2)); the general law of vicarious liability still applies`
        IF `an employee or akin to one`
           AND `the court finds the institution placed them in a position that gave occasion for the abuse`
           AND `they used that occasion to abuse me`
            THEN `yes: the institution is vicariously liable for my injury from the abuse (s 17G(1)); any award under section 17E or 17F is taken into account (s 17G(4))`
        OTHERWISE `section 17G does not make the institution liable on these facts; the general law of vicarious liability still applies (s 17G(3))`
    WHERE
        `an employee or akin to one` MEANS
               `the abuser's role` EQUALS `an employee of the institution`
            OR `the abuser's role` EQUALS `someone who carried out activities that enabled the institution to perform its functions, intending to benefit it (for example a volunteer, or a priest or religious leader)`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Part 3A Division 4 -- who answers my claim: the proper respondent (arises only once I have sued an institution)`

-- s 17H(1): the institution "may nominate another person or entity as the proper respondent". (2): it may be nominated
-- "if the person or entity: (a) has the financial capacity to pay the amount of damages that could reasonably be expected
-- to be awarded ...; and (b) has the financial capacity to pay the amount of legal costs ...; and (c) consents to the
-- nomination ...; and (d) is capable of being sued." s 17J(1): "If a court is satisfied of the matters specified in
-- section 17H(2), the court must appoint the nominated person or entity as the proper respondent". (2): it assumes the
-- institution's liability (including for costs), is joined, conducts the proceeding, and may rely on the institution's
-- defences and rights of indemnity.
DECLARE `Must the court appoint the nominee` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court must appoint the nominee as the proper respondent; it takes on the institution's liability to me (including for costs), is joined and conducts the defence, and may rely on the institution's defences (s 17J)`
    `no: the court is not required to appoint the nominee, because it is not satisfied of every matter in section 17H(2); the claim stays against the institution`

@export The institution I sued in the Northern Territory for child abuse has nominated someone else as the "proper respondent". Must the court appoint them?
GIVEN `the court is satisfied they can pay the likely damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied the nominee has the financial capacity to pay the damages that could reasonably be expected to be awarded
      `the court is satisfied they can pay the likely costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied the nominee has the financial capacity to pay the legal costs that could reasonably be expected to be awarded against it
      `the nominee consents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the nominee consents to being nominated as the proper respondent
      `the nominee can be sued` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the nominee is capable of being sued
GIVETH A `Must the court appoint the nominee`
DECIDE `Must the court appoint the nominee question` IS
    IF `the court is satisfied they can pay the likely damages` AND `the court is satisfied they can pay the likely costs` AND `the nominee consents` AND `the nominee can be sued`
        THEN `yes: the court must appoint the nominee as the proper respondent; it takes on the institution's liability to me (including for costs), is joined and conducts the defence, and may rely on the institution's defences (s 17J)`
        ELSE `no: the court is not required to appoint the nominee, because it is not satisfied of every matter in section 17H(2); the claim stays against the institution`

-- s 17K(1): the claimant "may apply to the court for the court to appoint an associated trust of the institution as the
-- proper respondent in the proceeding if: (a) the institution: (i) did not nominate another person or entity as the proper
-- respondent ...; or (ii) nominated another person or entity ... and that person or entity was not appointed by the court
-- ...; and (b) at least 120 days from the day the proceeding was commenced have passed."
DECLARE `What happened to a nomination` IS ONE OF
    `the institution did not nominate a proper respondent`
    `the institution nominated one and the court did not appoint it`
    `the institution nominated one and the court has not yet decided`
    `the court appointed the proper respondent the institution nominated`

DECLARE `Can I apply to appoint an associated trust` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I may apply to the court to appoint an associated trust of the institution as the proper respondent (s 17K(1)); the institution must then, within 28 days, identify its associated trusts and their financial capacity (s 17K(3))` HAS `I could apply from` IS A DATE
    `not yet: I may apply once at least 120 days from the day the proceeding was commenced have passed (s 17K(1)(b))` HAS `I can apply from` IS A DATE
    `not yet: the court has not decided the institution's nomination (s 17K(1)(a)(ii))`
    `no: the court has appointed the proper respondent the institution nominated, so section 17K(1)(a) is not met`

-- "At least 120 days from the day the proceeding was commenced have passed": the day of commencement is not counted, so the
-- 120 days end at the end of the 120th day after it, and the first day they "have passed" is the 121st day after it.
GIVEN `the date the proceeding was commenced` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the first day I may apply under s 17K after a proceeding commenced on` `the date the proceeding was commenced` MEANS `the date` 121 `days after` `the date the proceeding was commenced`

@export I sued an institution in the Northern Territory for child abuse. Can I ask the court to make the institution's associated trust the defendant, and from when?
GIVEN `what happened to a nomination` IS A `What happened to a nomination` @desc Whether the institution nominated someone else as the proper respondent, and what the court did
      `the date the proceeding was commenced` IS A DATE @desc The date my proceeding against the institution was commenced (ISO 8601)
      `the date I would apply` IS A DATE @desc The date I would make the application, usually today (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Can I apply to appoint an associated trust`
DECIDE `Can I apply to appoint an associated trust question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `what happened to a nomination` EQUALS `the court appointed the proper respondent the institution nominated`
            THEN `no: the court has appointed the proper respondent the institution nominated, so section 17K(1)(a) is not met`
        IF `what happened to a nomination` EQUALS `the institution nominated one and the court has not yet decided`
            THEN `not yet: the court has not decided the institution's nomination (s 17K(1)(a)(ii))`
        IF `the date I would apply` `falls before` `the first day`
            THEN `not yet: I may apply once at least 120 days from the day the proceeding was commenced have passed (s 17K(1)(b))` `the first day`
        OTHERWISE `yes: I may apply to the court to appoint an associated trust of the institution as the proper respondent (s 17K(1)); the institution must then, within 28 days, identify its associated trusts and their financial capacity (s 17K(3))` `the first day`
    WHERE
        `the first day` MEANS `the first day I may apply under s 17K after a proceeding commenced on` `the date the proceeding was commenced`

-- s 17K(3): "Within 28 days from the day the application is made under subsection (1), the institution must identify for
-- the court all associated trusts of the institution (if any) and provide information regarding the financial capacity of
-- those associated trusts." A duty with a deadline, so a regulative rule. WITHIN counts days from the application (day 0).
DECLARE `Claim party` IS ONE OF
    `I, the claimant`
    `the institution`
    `the public entity defendant`

DECLARE `Claim action` IS ONE OF
    `identify its associated trusts and give the court information on their financial capacity`
    `give me the details I asked for about the money held on trust`

GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim action`
DECIDE `s 17K(3) -- the institution must identify its associated trusts within 28 days` IS
    PARTY `the institution`
    MUST  `identify its associated trusts and give the court information on their financial capacity`
    WITHIN 28
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `the institution` BECAUSE "the institution did not identify its associated trusts and their financial capacity within 28 days from the day the application was made (s 17K(3))"

@export I have applied to the court in the Northern Territory to appoint an institution's associated trust as the defendant to my child abuse claim. By when must the institution tell the court about its trusts?
GIVEN `the date I applied` IS A DATE @desc The date I made the application under section 17K(1) (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `By when must the institution identify its trusts question` IS `the date` 28 `days after` `the date I applied`

-- s 17K(4): "A trust is an associated trust of an institution in any of the following circumstances: (a) the institution
-- has, either directly or indirectly, the power to control the application of the income, or the distribution of the
-- property, of the trust; (b) ... the power to obtain the beneficial enjoyment of the property or income of the trust ...;
-- (c) ... the power to appoint or remove the trustee or trustees ...; (d) ... the power to appoint or remove beneficiaries
-- ...; (e) the trustee of the trust is accustomed or under an obligation, whether formal or informal, to act according to
-- the directions, instructions or wishes of the institution; (f) ... the power to determine the outcome of any other
-- decisions about the trust's operations; (g) a member of the institution or a management member of the institution has,
-- under the trust deed ..., a power of a kind referred to in paragraph (a), (b), (c), (d) or (f)."
DECLARE `Is it an associated trust` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the trust is an associated trust of the institution (s 17K(4))`
    `no: none of the circumstances in section 17K(4) applies, so the trust is not an associated trust of the institution`

@export Is a particular trust an "associated trust" of the institution I am suing in the Northern Territory for child abuse?
GIVEN `the institution controls its income or property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the institution has, directly or indirectly, power to control how the trust's income is applied or its property distributed
      `the institution can benefit from its property or income` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the institution has power to obtain the beneficial enjoyment of the trust's property or income, with or without anyone's consent
      `the institution appoints or removes its trustees` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the institution has, directly or indirectly, power to appoint or remove the trustees
      `the institution appoints or removes its beneficiaries` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the institution has, directly or indirectly, power to appoint or remove beneficiaries
      `the trustee follows the institution's wishes` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the trustee is accustomed or obliged, formally or informally, to act on the institution's directions, instructions or wishes
      `the institution decides the trust's other operations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the institution has, directly or indirectly, power to determine the outcome of any other decisions about the trust's operations
      `a member or management member has such a power under the trust deed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a member or a management member of the institution has, under the trust deed, any of the powers above (other than the trustee following the institution's wishes)
GIVETH A `Is it an associated trust`
DECIDE `Is it an associated trust question` IS
    IF    `the institution controls its income or property`
       OR `the institution can benefit from its property or income`
       OR `the institution appoints or removes its trustees`
       OR `the institution appoints or removes its beneficiaries`
       OR `the trustee follows the institution's wishes`
       OR `the institution decides the trust's other operations`
       OR `a member or management member has such a power under the trust deed`
        THEN `yes: the trust is an associated trust of the institution (s 17K(4))`
        ELSE `no: none of the circumstances in section 17K(4) applies, so the trust is not an associated trust of the institution`

-- s 17K(2): an associated trust may be appointed "if the associated trust: (a) has the financial capacity to pay the
-- amount of damages ...; and (b) ... legal costs ...; and (c) is capable of being sued." s 17L(1): "If a court is
-- satisfied of the matters specified in section 17K(2), the court must appoint the associated trust as the proper
-- respondent". s 17M(3): "Any liability of a trust that is a proper respondent is limited to the value of the trust
-- property."
DECLARE `Must the court appoint the trust` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court must appoint the associated trust as the proper respondent; it takes on the institution's liability to me (s 17L), but its liability is limited to the value of the trust property (s 17M(3))`
    `no: the court is not required to appoint the trust, because it is not satisfied of every matter in section 17K(2)`

@export I have asked the court in the Northern Territory to appoint an institution's associated trust as the defendant to my child abuse claim. Must the court appoint it?
GIVEN `the court is satisfied the trust can pay the likely damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied the trust has the financial capacity to pay the damages that could reasonably be expected to be awarded
      `the court is satisfied the trust can pay the likely costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied the trust has the financial capacity to pay the legal costs that could reasonably be expected to be awarded
      `the trust can be sued` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the trust (through its trustee) is capable of being sued
GIVETH A `Must the court appoint the trust`
DECIDE `Must the court appoint the trust question` IS
    IF `the court is satisfied the trust can pay the likely damages` AND `the court is satisfied the trust can pay the likely costs` AND `the trust can be sued`
        THEN `yes: the court must appoint the associated trust as the proper respondent; it takes on the institution's liability to me (s 17L), but its liability is limited to the value of the trust property (s 17M(3))`
        ELSE `no: the court is not required to appoint the trust, because it is not satisfied of every matter in section 17K(2)`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Part 3A Divisions 5, 6 -- the institution has changed or is unincorporated (arises only in those situations)`

-- s 17P(2): "A successor institution assumes the liability of the former institution for the claim if the successor
-- institution is substantially the same as the former institution." (3): "Without limiting subsection (2)", it is
-- substantially the same where (a) the former (or part) merged into it; (b) merged with others to form it; (c) it is the
-- remainder after part left; (d) a chain of such steps. (4): a change of name, structure, incorporation or geographic
-- area is "not relevant to whether an institution is a successor institution".
DECLARE `What happened to the institution` IS ONE OF
    `it, or part of it, merged into another institution`
    `it, or part of it, merged with one or more other institutions to form a new one`
    `the other institution is what remains of it after part of it left`
    `a chain of such mergers and separations leads from it to the other institution`
    `it only changed its name or structure, became incorporated, or changed the area where it works`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Is the successor liable` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the successor institution is substantially the same as the former one and assumes its liability for my claim (s 17P(2), (3))`
    `yes: a change of name, structure or area, or becoming incorporated, does not matter; my claim is against the institution as it now is (s 17P(4))`
    `yes, if the court finds the other institution substantially the same as the former one (s 17P(2)); section 17P(3) does not list what happened`

@export The institution that had care of me when I was abused as a child in the Northern Territory has merged, split or changed. Is the institution that succeeded it liable?
GIVEN `what happened to the institution` IS A `What happened to the institution` @desc What happened to the institution after the abuse
GIVETH A `Is the successor liable`
DECIDE `Is the successor liable question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `what happened to the institution` EQUALS `it only changed its name or structure, became incorporated, or changed the area where it works`
            THEN `yes: a change of name, structure or area, or becoming incorporated, does not matter; my claim is against the institution as it now is (s 17P(4))`
        IF `what happened to the institution` EQUALS `something else`
            THEN `yes, if the court finds the other institution substantially the same as the former one (s 17P(2)); section 17P(3) does not list what happened`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the successor institution is substantially the same as the former one and assumes its liability for my claim (s 17P(2), (3))`

-- s 17Q(1): a proceeding "may be commenced or continued against an unincorporated institution in the name of the
-- institution as if the institution were a legal person capable of being sued." s 17R(1): applies if "(a) the claimant
-- was subject to child abuse by an individual associated with an institution while the claimant was a child under the
-- care, supervision or authority of the institution; and (b) the claimant has or had an accrued cause of action ...
-- against a person who held office as a management member of the institution at the time the cause of action accrued (the
-- former office holder); and (c) at the time of the accrual ... the institution was not incorporated; and (d) the
-- institution is not incorporated; and (e) the former office holder no longer holds office ...; and (f) the claimant would
-- be able to maintain an action on the cause of action if the former office holder continued to hold office." (2): then
-- the proceeding "may be commenced or continued against a current office holder in the name of the office of the former
-- office holder." (3): liability, acts, duties, indemnities and defences pass to the current office holder. (4): with no
-- equivalent office, the head of the institution.
DECLARE `Whom can I sue` IS ONE OF
    `the institution itself: Division 6 of Part 3A is for unincorporated institutions, and this one is incorporated`
    `I may sue the institution in its own name, as if it were a legal person (s 17Q(1))`
    `I may sue the institution in its own name (s 17Q(1)); and I may sue the current holder of the former office holder's office, in the name of that office, who takes on the former office holder's liability and defences (s 17R(2), (3))`
    `I may sue the institution in its own name (s 17Q(1)); and I may sue the head of the institution in the name of the former office holder's office, who takes on the former office holder's liability and defences (s 17R(2)-(4))`

@export The institution that had care of me when I was abused as a child in the Northern Territory is not incorporated. Whom can I sue?
GIVEN `the institution is incorporated now` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the institution is now incorporated (for example as an incorporated association or company)
      `I was abused by someone associated with it while in its care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I was abused as a child by an individual associated with the institution while under its care, supervision or authority
      `I had a claim against a management member at the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I have or had an accrued cause of action for the injury against a person who was a management member (for example on its management committee) when the cause of action accrued
      `it was unincorporated when my claim accrued` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the institution was unincorporated when the cause of action accrued
      `that person no longer holds the office` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that former office holder no longer holds office as a management member
      `I could sue them if they still held office` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I would be able to maintain the action if that person still held office
      `the institution has an equivalent office now` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the institution now has an office equivalent to the one the former office holder held
GIVETH A `Whom can I sue`
DECIDE `Whom can I sue question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the institution is incorporated now`
            THEN `the institution itself: Division 6 of Part 3A is for unincorporated institutions, and this one is incorporated`
        IF (NOT `section 17R applies`)
            THEN `I may sue the institution in its own name, as if it were a legal person (s 17Q(1))`
        IF `the institution has an equivalent office now`
            THEN `I may sue the institution in its own name (s 17Q(1)); and I may sue the current holder of the former office holder's office, in the name of that office, who takes on the former office holder's liability and defences (s 17R(2), (3))`
        OTHERWISE `I may sue the institution in its own name (s 17Q(1)); and I may sue the head of the institution in the name of the former office holder's office, who takes on the former office holder's liability and defences (s 17R(2)-(4))`
    WHERE
        `section 17R applies` MEANS
                `I was abused by someone associated with it while in its care`
            AND `I had a claim against a management member at the time`
            AND `it was unincorporated when my claim accrued`
            AND `that person no longer holds the office`
            AND `I could sue them if they still held office`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Part 4 -- how much can I get`

-- s 19: "A court must not award aggravated damages or exemplary damages in respect of a personal injury." s 32F: the same
-- "in respect of a civil wrong to which this Part [4A] applies".
DECLARE `My claim is` IS ONE OF
    `a claim for damages for personal injury to which this Act applies`
    `a claim, as a person in custody, for assault, battery or false imprisonment to which Part 4A applies`

DECLARE `Can I get aggravated or exemplary damages` IS ONE OF
    `no: a court must not award aggravated or exemplary damages for a personal injury (s 19)`
    `no: a court must not award aggravated or exemplary damages for a civil wrong to which Part 4A applies (s 32F)`

@export Can I be awarded aggravated or exemplary (punitive) damages on my claim in the Northern Territory?
GIVEN `my claim is` IS A `My claim is` @desc What my claim is
GIVETH A `Can I get aggravated or exemplary damages`
DECIDE `Can I get aggravated or exemplary damages question` IS
    IF `my claim is` EQUALS `a claim for damages for personal injury to which this Act applies`
        THEN `no: a court must not award aggravated or exemplary damages for a personal injury (s 19)`
        ELSE `no: a court must not award aggravated or exemplary damages for a civil wrong to which Part 4A applies (s 32F)`

-- s 20: in assessing damages for past or future loss of earnings or earning capacity, or loss of expectation of financial
-- support, "a court must disregard the amount (if any) by which the injured person's gross weekly earnings would, but for
-- the personal injury, have exceeded an amount that is 3 times average weekly earnings as published before 1 January
-- preceding the date on which the assessment is made." s 18: "average weekly earnings means the Average Weekly Earnings
-- for Full Time Adult Persons, Weekly Ordinary Time Earnings for the Northern Territory as estimated and published by the
-- Australian Statistician." The figure is published outside the Act and is an input.
@export My lost earnings are being assessed under the Northern Territory Personal Injuries (Liabilities and Damages) Act. What is the most weekly income the court can count?
GIVEN `my gross weekly earnings but for the injury` IS A NUMBER @desc What my gross weekly earnings would have been but for the injury, in dollars
      `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc The Australian Statistician's Average Weekly Earnings for Full Time Adult Persons, Weekly Ordinary Time Earnings, for the Northern Territory, as last published before 1 January preceding the assessment, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `The weekly earnings the court may count question` IS
    min `my gross weekly earnings but for the injury` (3 TIMES `average weekly earnings`)

-- s 21(1): "A court may award damages for future pecuniary loss only if satisfied by the claimant that the assumptions
-- about the injured person's future earning capacity, or the occurrence of other events on which the award is to be
-- based, accord with the injured person's most likely future circumstances had the personal injury not occurred." (2):
-- "it must adjust the amount ... by reference to the percentage possibility that the events might have occurred
-- regardless of the personal injury." s 22(1): a lump sum for future pecuniary loss "is to be assessed in accordance with
-- discounted present values"; (2): "the discount rate ... prescribed by the Regulations; or (b) if no discount rate is
-- prescribed by the Regulations -- the discount rate of 5%." No Regulations are deposited; 5% is used.
DECLARE `My future loss` IS ONE OF
    `no damages for future pecuniary loss: the court is not satisfied my assumptions accord with my most likely future had I not been injured (s 21(1))`
    `the amount, adjusted for the possibility that the events would have happened anyway, then discounted to present value by an actuarial multiplier at the discount rate (ss 21(2), 22)` HAS `the adjusted amount before discounting` IS A NUMBER, `the discount rate in percent` IS A NUMBER

@export How will my future financial loss be worked out under the Northern Territory Personal Injuries (Liabilities and Damages) Act?
GIVEN `the court is satisfied with my assumptions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that my assumptions about my future earning capacity or other events accord with my most likely future circumstances had I not been injured
      `the future loss on those assumptions` IS A NUMBER @desc The future loss assessed on those assumptions, before any adjustment or discount, in dollars
      `the percentage possibility it would have happened anyway` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage possibility that the events would have occurred regardless of the injury (for example 20 for 20%)
GIVETH A `My future loss`
DECIDE `My future loss question` IS
    IF `the court is satisfied with my assumptions`
        THEN `the amount, adjusted for the possibility that the events would have happened anyway, then discounted to present value by an actuarial multiplier at the discount rate (ss 21(2), 22)` (`the future loss on those assumptions` MINUS ((`the future loss on those assumptions` TIMES `the percentage possibility it would have happened anyway`) DIVIDED BY 100)) 5
        ELSE `no damages for future pecuniary loss: the court is not satisfied my assumptions accord with my most likely future had I not been injured (s 21(1))`

-- s 23(1): damages for gratuitous services "only if the court is satisfied: (a) that there is or was a reasonable need for
-- the services; (b) that the need for the services is or was solely because of the personal injury ...; and (c) that the
-- services would not be provided ... but for the personal injury". (2): "only if the services are provided or are to be
-- provided: (a) for 6 hours or more per week; and (b) for 6 months or more." (3): for 40 or more hours a week, not to
-- exceed average weekly earnings (for the quarter, or the most recent published quarter). (4): for less than 40 hours,
-- "not to exceed the amount calculated at an hourly rate of one-fortieth" of that amount. s 29: no interest on them.
-- s 18: "gratuitous services means attendant care services provided, or to be provided, to an injured person and for which
-- payment is not required"; "attendant care services" are domestic, nursing, or alleviating services "required for the
-- essential and regular care of an injured person".
DECLARE `Can I get damages for free care` IS ONE OF
    `no: the court is not satisfied that the need was reasonable, solely because of the injury, and that the care would not otherwise be given (s 23(1))`
    `no: damages for gratuitous services need at least 6 hours of care a week (s 23(2)(a))`
    `no: damages for gratuitous services need care for at least 6 months (s 23(2)(b))`
    `yes: damages for the care, up to a weekly limit, with no interest on them (ss 23(3), (4), 29)` HAS `the most per week of care` IS A NUMBER

@export My family or friends care for me for free because of my injury in the Northern Territory. Can I get damages for that care, and how much at most?
GIVEN `the court is satisfied the need is reasonable and due only to the injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied there is a reasonable need for the care, the need is solely because of the injury, and the care would not be given but for the injury
      `hours of care per week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours a week of care are (or will be) given
      `months of care` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many months the care is (or will be) given
      `average weekly earnings for the quarter` IS A NUMBER @desc The Northern Territory Average Weekly Earnings (Full Time Adult Persons, Ordinary Time) for the quarter in which the care was given, or for future care the most recent quarter published before the award, in dollars
GIVETH A `Can I get damages for free care`
DECIDE `Can I get damages for free care question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `the court is satisfied the need is reasonable and due only to the injury`)
            THEN `no: the court is not satisfied that the need was reasonable, solely because of the injury, and that the care would not otherwise be given (s 23(1))`
        IF `hours of care per week` LESS THAN 6
            THEN `no: damages for gratuitous services need at least 6 hours of care a week (s 23(2)(a))`
        IF `months of care` LESS THAN 6
            THEN `no: damages for gratuitous services need care for at least 6 months (s 23(2)(b))`
        IF `hours of care per week` AT LEAST 40
            THEN `yes: damages for the care, up to a weekly limit, with no interest on them (ss 23(3), (4), 29)` `average weekly earnings for the quarter`
        OTHERWISE `yes: damages for the care, up to a weekly limit, with no interest on them (ss 23(3), (4), 29)` ((`average weekly earnings for the quarter` DIVIDED BY 40) TIMES `hours of care per week`)

-- s 25: damages other than for pecuniary loss only in accordance with s 27, after the degree of permanent impairment is
-- determined under s 26 (on a medical practitioner's assessment under the prescribed guides). s 27(1): "The maximum ... is
-- 680 000 monetary units." (2): "A court must not award damages for non-pecuniary loss if the court determines the degree
-- of permanent impairment to be less than 5% of the whole person." (3): "a court must award the following amount: (a) if
-- ... 85% or more ... -- 680 000 monetary units; (b) if ... not less than 15% and not more than 84% ... -- the relevant
-- percentage of 680 000 monetary units; (c) if ... a percentage ... specified in column 1 of the Table -- the amount
-- specified in column 2". Table: not less than 5% but less than 10%: 13 600; 10%: 20 400; 11%: 27 200; 12%: 40 800;
-- 13%: 54 400; 14%: 81 600 monetary units.
`the maximum for non-pecuniary loss` MEANS 680000

@export What will I be awarded for pain and suffering (non-pecuniary loss) under the Northern Territory Personal Injuries (Liabilities and Damages) Act? (The answer is in monetary units.)
GIVEN `my degree of permanent impairment` IS A NUMBER @desc My degree of permanent impairment as a percentage of the whole person, as the court determines it on a medical practitioner's assessment under the prescribed guides (for example 12 for 12%)
GIVETH AN `Answer` NUMBER
DECIDE `My damages for non-pecuniary loss question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `my degree of permanent impairment` LESS THAN 5
            THEN `answered` 0
        IF `my degree of permanent impairment` LESS THAN 10
            THEN `answered` 13600
        IF `my degree of permanent impairment` EQUALS 10
            THEN `answered` 20400
        IF `my degree of permanent impairment` EQUALS 11
            THEN `answered` 27200
        IF `my degree of permanent impairment` EQUALS 12
            THEN `answered` 40800
        IF `my degree of permanent impairment` EQUALS 13
            THEN `answered` 54400
        IF `my degree of permanent impairment` EQUALS 14
            THEN `answered` 81600
        IF `my degree of permanent impairment` LESS THAN 15
            THEN `not covered` `the Table in section 27(3)(c) lists whole percentages from 10% to 14%, and section 27(3)(b) starts at 15%; a degree between two of them is not provided for`
        IF `my degree of permanent impairment` AT MOST 84
            THEN `answered` ((`the maximum for non-pecuniary loss` TIMES `my degree of permanent impairment`) DIVIDED BY 100)
        IF `my degree of permanent impairment` LESS THAN 85
            THEN `not covered` `section 27(3)(b) ends at 84% and section 27(3)(a) starts at 85%; a degree between them is not provided for`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `the maximum for non-pecuniary loss`

-- s 29: "A court must not order the payment of interest on damages awarded for: (a) non-pecuniary loss; or (b) gratuitous
-- services." s 30(1): "If a court is satisfied that interest is payable on damages, the amount of interest: (a) is payable
-- in respect of the period from when the relevant loss was first incurred until the date on which the court assesses the
-- damages; and (b) is to be calculated, using the prescribed rate of interest". (2): the rate prescribed by the
-- Regulations, or if none, "the relevant interest rate as at the date of assessment": the Commonwealth Government 10 year
-- benchmark bond rate published by the Reserve Bank "as applying: (a) on the first business day of January of each year in
-- respect of an assessment of damages during the period from 1 March until 31 August of that year; or (b) on the first
-- business day of July of each year in respect of an assessment of damages made during the period from 1 September of
-- that year until the last day of February of the following year." No Regulations are deposited.
DECLARE `The damages are for` IS ONE OF
    `non-pecuniary loss (pain and suffering)`
    `gratuitous services (free care)`
    `another kind of loss (for example lost earnings or medical expenses)`

DECLARE `The bond rate month` IS ONE OF `January`, `July`

DECLARE `Will I get interest` IS ONE OF
    `no: a court must not order interest on damages for non-pecuniary loss or gratuitous services (s 29)`
    `no: interest is payable only if the court is satisfied it is payable (s 30(1))`
    `yes: interest for the period from when the loss was first incurred until the assessment, at the 10-year Commonwealth bond rate the Reserve Bank published as applying on the first business day of the month and year given, unless the Regulations prescribe a rate (s 30)` HAS `interest runs from` IS A DATE, `interest runs until` IS A DATE, `the rate published for the first business day of` IS A `The bond rate month`, `of the year` IS A NUMBER

@export Will I get interest on my damages in the Northern Territory, for what period, and at what rate?
GIVEN `the damages are for` IS A `The damages are for` @desc Which kind of damages I am asking about
      `the court is satisfied interest is payable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that interest is payable on those damages
      `the date the loss was first incurred` IS A DATE @desc The date the loss was first incurred (ISO 8601)
      `the date the court assesses the damages` IS A DATE @desc The date the court assesses the damages (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Will I get interest`
DECIDE `Will I get interest question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`the damages are for` EQUALS `another kind of loss (for example lost earnings or medical expenses)`))
            THEN `no: a court must not order interest on damages for non-pecuniary loss or gratuitous services (s 29)`
        IF (NOT `the court is satisfied interest is payable`)
            THEN `no: interest is payable only if the court is satisfied it is payable (s 30(1))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: interest for the period from when the loss was first incurred until the assessment, at the 10-year Commonwealth bond rate the Reserve Bank published as applying on the first business day of the month and year given, unless the Regulations prescribe a rate (s 30)` `the date the loss was first incurred` `the date the court assesses the damages` `the bond month` `the bond year`
    WHERE
        `the month` MEANS `the month of` `the date the court assesses the damages`
        `the year` MEANS `the year of` `the date the court assesses the damages`
        `the bond month` MEANS
            IF `the month` AT LEAST 3 AND `the month` AT MOST 8
                THEN `January`
                ELSE `July`
        `the bond year` MEANS
            IF `the month` AT MOST 2
                THEN `the year` MINUS 1
                ELSE `the year`

-- s 31: "structured settlement means an order providing for the payment of all or part of an award of damages by" periodic
-- payments funded by an annuity or other agreed means, or periodic payments of future reasonable medical, hospital,
-- pharmaceutical or attendant care expenses as incurred. s 32: "The court may, with the consent of the parties to a
-- proceeding, make an order for a structured settlement." ss 4(2), (3), 35(3): this applies to every claim, whenever the
-- incident and whenever the proceeding began.
DECLARE `Can my damages be paid in instalments` IS ONE OF
    `yes: with the consent of all the parties, the court may order a structured settlement: periodic payments funded by an annuity or other agreed means, or periodic payments of future medical, hospital, pharmaceutical or attendant care expenses as they are incurred (ss 31, 32)`
    `no: the court may order a structured settlement only with the consent of the parties (s 32)`

@export Can my personal injury damages in the Northern Territory be paid as a structured settlement (periodic payments) instead of a lump sum?
GIVEN `all the parties consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the parties to the proceeding consent to a structured settlement
GIVETH A `Can my damages be paid in instalments`
DECIDE `Can my damages be paid in instalments question` IS
    IF `all the parties consent`
        THEN `yes: with the consent of all the parties, the court may order a structured settlement: periodic payments funded by an annuity or other agreed means, or periodic payments of future medical, hospital, pharmaceutical or attendant care expenses as they are incurred (ss 31, 32)`
        ELSE `no: the court may order a structured settlement only with the consent of the parties (s 32)`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Part 4A -- I was assaulted, battered or falsely imprisoned while in custody (arises only if I was a prisoner or detainee)`

-- s 32B(1): Part 4A applies to damages or a settlement "to an individual for a civil wrong the individual sustains when the
-- individual is: (a) an offender within the meaning of section 5 of the Correctional Services Act 2014; or (b) a detainee
-- as defined in section 5(1) of the Youth Justice Act 2005." (2): not to a Return to Work Act claim by an offender who was
-- a worker. (3): "this Part does not apply to any claim for damages for a personal injury." (4): it applies to "(a) the
-- tort of assault; (b) the tort of battery; (c) the tort of false imprisonment." (5): not to a civil wrong from an
-- incident before the commencement of s 32B. s 32A: "civil wrong means a civil wrong to which section 32B applies." s 32D:
-- "Nothing in this Part affects a claim for a civil wrong arising from child abuse perpetrated against an offender."
DECLARE `The wrong done to me` IS ONE OF
    `assault by strip searching me without lawful reason`
    `another assault`
    `battery`
    `false imprisonment`
    `another civil wrong (for example negligence)`

DECLARE `Does Part 4A apply to my claim` IS ONE OF
    `yes: Part 4A applies: my damages are capped (s 32E), there are no aggravated or exemplary damages (s 32F), a public entity defendant may withhold what I owe for victims levies and fines (s 32G), and the rest is held on trust by the Public Trustee for victims' claims before it is released to me (ss 32H, 32M)`
    `no: Part 4A does not affect a claim for a civil wrong arising from child abuse perpetrated against me (s 32D)`
    `no: Part 4A does not apply to a claim for damages for personal injury (s 32B(3)); the rest of the Act applies to that claim`
    `no: Part 4A does not apply to a workers compensation claim by an offender who was a worker (s 32B(2))`
    `no: Part 4A applies only to a wrong sustained while I was an offender under the Correctional Services Act 2014 or a detainee under the Youth Justice Act 2005 (s 32B(1), 32E(3))`
    `no: Part 4A applies only to assault, battery and false imprisonment (ss 32A, 32B(4))`
    `no: Part 4A does not apply to a wrong from an incident before section 32B commenced (s 32B(5))`

@export I was assaulted, battered or falsely imprisoned while a prisoner or youth detainee in the Northern Territory. Does Part 4A (damages awarded to offenders) apply to my claim?
GIVEN `the wrong done to me` IS A `The wrong done to me` @desc What the civil wrong done to me was
      `I was a prisoner or detainee at the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when the wrong was done, I was an offender under section 5 of the Correctional Services Act 2014 (for example a prisoner, or a person under a supervision order) or a detainee under the Youth Justice Act 2005
      `my claim is for personal injury damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether my claim is for damages for a personal injury (including a psychological injury) rather than for the wrong itself
      `my claim is for workers compensation as a worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether my claim is for compensation under the Return to Work Act 1986 for an injury sustained while I was a worker
      `the wrong arose from child abuse against me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the wrong arose from child abuse (sexual abuse, serious physical abuse, or psychological abuse arising from either) perpetrated against me as a child
      `the date of the incident` IS A DATE @desc The date of the incident (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Does Part 4A apply to my claim`
DECIDE `Does Part 4A apply to my claim question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the wrong arose from child abuse against me`
            THEN `no: Part 4A does not affect a claim for a civil wrong arising from child abuse perpetrated against me (s 32D)`
        IF `my claim is for workers compensation as a worker`
            THEN `no: Part 4A does not apply to a workers compensation claim by an offender who was a worker (s 32B(2))`
        IF `my claim is for personal injury damages`
            THEN `no: Part 4A does not apply to a claim for damages for personal injury (s 32B(3)); the rest of the Act applies to that claim`
        IF (NOT `I was a prisoner or detainee at the time`)
            THEN `no: Part 4A applies only to a wrong sustained while I was an offender under the Correctional Services Act 2014 or a detainee under the Youth Justice Act 2005 (s 32B(1), 32E(3))`
        IF `the wrong done to me` EQUALS `another civil wrong (for example negligence)`
            THEN `no: Part 4A applies only to assault, battery and false imprisonment (ss 32A, 32B(4))`
        IF `the date of the incident` `falls before` `the commencement of section 32B`
            THEN `no: Part 4A does not apply to a wrong from an incident before section 32B commenced (s 32B(5))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: Part 4A applies: my damages are capped (s 32E), there are no aggravated or exemplary damages (s 32F), a public entity defendant may withhold what I owe for victims levies and fines (s 32G), and the rest is held on trust by the Public Trustee for victims' claims before it is released to me (ss 32H, 32M)`

-- s 32E(1): the maximum "for each civil wrong sustained by an offender": (a) assault: (i) by strip searching without
-- lawful reason, 5 000; (ii) any other, 2 500; (b) battery, 5 000; (c) false imprisonment: (i) "for a period of 1 day or
-- less -- 2 000"; (ii) "more than 1 day and less than 30 days -- 1000 monetary units for each day during which the false
-- imprisonment continues"; (iii) "30 days or more and less than 60 days -- 500 ... for each day"; (iv) "60 days or more --
-- 250 ... for each day". (2): "The maximum amount of damages a court may award to an offender for a civil wrong or series
-- of related civil wrongs to which this Part applies is 15 000 monetary units." (4): "series of related civil wrongs means
-- 2 or more civil wrongs ... that: (a) occur at approximately the same time; or (b) occur over a period of time and are
-- committed by the same person or group of persons."
`the overall maximum under s 32E(2)` MEANS 15000

GIVEN `the wrong done to me` IS A `The wrong done to me`, `the length of the false imprisonment in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 32E(1) maximum for` `the wrong done to me` `lasting` `the length of the false imprisonment in days` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the wrong done to me`
    WHEN `assault by strip searching me without lawful reason` THEN 5000
    WHEN `another assault` THEN 2500
    WHEN `battery` THEN 5000
    WHEN `false imprisonment` THEN
        BRANCH
            IF `the length of the false imprisonment in days` AT MOST 1
                THEN 2000
            IF `the length of the false imprisonment in days` LESS THAN 30
                THEN 1000 TIMES `whole days in` `the length of the false imprisonment in days`
            IF `the length of the false imprisonment in days` LESS THAN 60
                THEN 500 TIMES `whole days in` `the length of the false imprisonment in days`
            OTHERWISE 250 TIMES `whole days in` `the length of the false imprisonment in days`
    OTHERWISE 0

@export As a prisoner or detainee in the Northern Territory, what is the most I can be awarded for one assault, battery or false imprisonment under Part 4A? (The answer is in monetary units.)
GIVEN `the wrong done to me` IS A `The wrong done to me` @desc Which civil wrong (Part 4A covers only assault, battery and false imprisonment)
      `the length of the false imprisonment in days` IS A NUMBER @desc Only for false imprisonment: how long it lasted, in days (part of a day counts as a day for the per-day amounts); enter 0 for other wrongs
GIVETH AN `Answer` NUMBER
DECIDE `The most for one wrong question` IS
    `answered` (min (`the s 32E(1) maximum for` `the wrong done to me` `lasting` `the length of the false imprisonment in days`) `the overall maximum under s 32E(2)`)

@export As a prisoner or detainee in the Northern Territory, what is the most I can be awarded for a series of related assaults, batteries or false imprisonments under Part 4A? (The answer is in monetary units.)
GIVEN `the total the court would otherwise award` IS A NUMBER @desc The total, in monetary units, of what the court would otherwise award for all the related wrongs (2 or more at about the same time, or over a period by the same person or group), each within its own maximum
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `The most for a series of related wrongs question` IS
    min `the total the court would otherwise award` `the overall maximum under s 32E(2)`

-- s 32G(1): a public entity defendant ordered to pay damages "may withhold an amount from the damages ... for the
-- satisfaction of the following amounts owed by the offender: (a) an amount the offender is required to pay under section
-- 55F of the Victims of Crime Assistance Act 2006 or an order made under section 56 of that Act; (b) an outstanding fine
-- ... and enforcement costs ...; (c) a penalty under an infringement notice ... not paid ..., and enforcement costs". (3):
-- not amounts cancelled, withdrawn or otherwise no longer recoverable. s 32C: who is a public entity defendant (an input).
-- s 32H(1): the damages "must be paid to the Public Trustee" (any defendant: NOTES.md, finding 10); (2): held on trust
-- from the day of the award or settlement "and ending on the later of: (a) the day 12 months after the day the court
-- awards the damages ...; or (b) the day a court finally determines any victim claim that is commenced against the
-- offender: (i) before the day the court awards the damages ...; or (ii) within 12 months after" it. (4): not the amount
-- withheld under s 32G. s 32M: "On the expiry of the period ..., the Public Trustee must release to the offender" what
-- remains. s 32L(2): the Public Trustee's fees come out of it.
DECLARE `When the trust ends` IS ONE OF
    `the trust period ends on the date given; what remains is then released to me (s 32M)` HAS `the trust period ends on` IS A DATE
    `the trust period ends on the later of the date given and the day the victim claims are finally determined; what remains is then released to me (s 32M)` HAS `not before` IS A DATE

DECLARE `What happens to my damages` HAS
    `the most the public entity may withhold for what I owe` IS A NUMBER
    `paid to the Public Trustee to hold on trust` IS A NUMBER
    `when the trust ends` IS A `When the trust ends`

@export As a prisoner or detainee in the Northern Territory I have been awarded damages (or a settlement) for assault, battery or false imprisonment. How much can be held back, and when will I get the money?
GIVEN `the damages awarded to me` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages awarded to me, or the money payable under the settlement, in dollars
      `the defendant is a public entity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant is a public entity defendant (the Territory, an Agency, a public sector employee, a health service, a government owned or controlled entity, or a person exercising statutory functions or acting for one of them)
      `what I owe for victims levies and orders` IS A NUMBER @desc What I still owe under section 55F of the Victims of Crime Assistance Act 2006 or an order under section 56 of that Act, in dollars
      `what I owe in unpaid fines and penalties` IS A NUMBER @desc What I still owe in outstanding court fines and unpaid infringement penalties, with enforcement costs, under the Fines and Penalties (Recovery) Act 2001, not counting any cancelled or no longer recoverable, in dollars
      `the date of the award or settlement` IS A DATE @desc The date the court awarded the damages, or the settlement was agreed (ISO 8601)
      `a victim claim against me began before or within 12 months after it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone has started (or starts) a court claim against me for an injury I caused by an offence, before the award or settlement or within 12 months after it
GIVETH A `What happens to my damages`
DECIDE `What happens to my damages question` IS
    `What happens to my damages` WITH
        `the most the public entity may withhold for what I owe` IS `the withholding`
        `paid to the Public Trustee to hold on trust` IS `the damages awarded to me` MINUS `the withholding`
        `when the trust ends` IS `the end of the trust`
    WHERE
        `the withholding` MEANS
            IF `the defendant is a public entity`
                THEN min `the damages awarded to me` (`what I owe for victims levies and orders` PLUS `what I owe in unpaid fines and penalties`)
                ELSE 0
        `twelve months on` MEANS `the date` 12 `months after` `the date of the award or settlement`
        `the end of the trust` MEANS
            IF `a victim claim against me began before or within 12 months after it`
                THEN `the trust period ends on the later of the date given and the day the victim claims are finally determined; what remains is then released to me (s 32M)` `twelve months on`
                ELSE `the trust period ends on the date given; what remains is then released to me (s 32M)` `twelve months on`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Part 4A Divisions 4, 5 -- the offender who hurt me has been awarded damages (arises only if I am the victim of an offender whose damages are held on trust)`

-- s 32A: "victim claim means a claim by an individual for personal injury against an offender that arises out of an
-- injury to the individual or death of the individual caused by the offender and the conduct causing the injury or death,
-- on the balance of probabilities, constitutes an offence." s 32P(1): "Despite section 12(1) of the Limitation Act 1981,
-- the limitation period under that section does not apply to an action founded on a tort that is a victim claim if there
-- exists money held on trust in accordance with section 32H in respect of the defendant to the victim claim." (2): a
-- cause of action barred by s 12(1) "is reinstated as maintainable" for commencing the claim and for an order under
-- s 32K(2). s 32K(2): if I succeed in, or settle, a victim claim, "the court may order the damages ... to be satisfied out
-- of the amount held on trust ... not exceeding the amount held on trust"; (3): not until all victim claims commenced
-- before or during the trust period are finally determined; (4): fair and reasonable having regard to other victims'
-- claims; (6): normally without a hearing.
DECLARE `Can I claim against the offender's damages` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Limitation Act 1981 section 12(1) period does not bar my claim while money is held on trust for the offender (s 32P); if I succeed or settle, the court may order my damages paid from the trust, up to what is held, once all victims' claims are finally determined, in a fair and reasonable share (s 32K)`
    `section 32P does not help: my claim is not a victim claim, because the conduct that injured me was not shown to be an offence (s 32A)`
    `section 32P does not help: no money is held on trust for the offender under section 32H, so the ordinary limitation period applies`

@export The offender who injured me in the Northern Territory has been awarded damages that are held on trust. Can I still sue them even if I am out of time, and be paid from that money?
GIVEN `the conduct that injured me was an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offender's conduct that injured me (or killed the person I claim for) constitutes an offence, on the balance of probabilities
      `money is held on trust for the offender` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether damages or settlement money awarded to the offender are currently held on trust by the Public Trustee under section 32H
GIVETH A `Can I claim against the offender's damages`
DECIDE `Can I claim against the offender's damages question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `the conduct that injured me was an offence`)
            THEN `section 32P does not help: my claim is not a victim claim, because the conduct that injured me was not shown to be an offence (s 32A)`
        IF (NOT `money is held on trust for the offender`)
            THEN `section 32P does not help: no money is held on trust for the offender under section 32H, so the ordinary limitation period applies`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the Limitation Act 1981 section 12(1) period does not bar my claim while money is held on trust for the offender (s 32P); if I succeed or settle, the court may order my damages paid from the trust, up to what is held, once all victims' claims are finally determined, in a fair and reasonable share (s 32K)`

-- s 32J(4): "A victim who is notified under subsection (1)(a) may, at any time during the period the damages or monies are
-- held on trust, request the public entity defendant to provide: (a) details of the amount ... held on trust and the
-- offence for which the offender has been found guilty; and (b) the number of other victims notified ... and whether any
-- orders ... were made under section 32K(2)." (5): "On a request under subsection (4), the public entity defendant must
-- provide to the victim, within a period of 28 days from the request, the details requested." A duty with a deadline, so a
-- regulative rule. WITHIN counts days from the request (day 0).
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim action`
DECIDE `s 32J(5) -- the public entity must give me the details within 28 days` IS
    PARTY `the public entity defendant`
    MUST  `give me the details I asked for about the money held on trust`
    WITHIN 28
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `the public entity defendant` BECAUSE "the public entity defendant did not give the victim the details requested within 28 days from the request (s 32J(5))"

DECLARE `Must they give me the details` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the public entity defendant must give me the amount held on trust, the offence, the number of other victims notified and any orders made, by the date given (s 32J(4), (5))` HAS `the details are due by` IS A DATE
    `no: a victim may ask only during the period the money is held on trust (s 32J(4))`

@export I was notified that an offender who hurt me in the Northern Territory has money held on trust, and I asked the public entity for details. Must they tell me, and by when?
GIVEN `I was notified in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the public entity defendant notified me in writing, as a victim, of the money held on trust (s 32J(1)(a))
      `the money was still held on trust when I asked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I asked while the money was still held on trust
      `the date of my request` IS A DATE @desc The date I asked the public entity defendant for the details (ISO 8601)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Must they give me the details`
DECIDE `Must they give me the details question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `I was notified in writing`)
            THEN `not covered` `only a victim notified in writing under section 32J(1)(a) is given the right to ask for details (s 32J(4)); the Act does not speak to others`
        IF (NOT `the money was still held on trust when I asked`)
            THEN `answered` `no: a victim may ask only during the period the money is held on trust (s 32J(4))`
        OTHERWISE `answered` (`yes: the public entity defendant must give me the amount held on trust, the offence, the number of other victims notified and any orders made, by the date given (s 32J(4), (5))` (`the date` 28 `days after` `the date of my request`))
